TY  - THES
T1  - Leveraging Generative Artifical Intelligence Large Language Models for Phishing Email Detection: Empirical Assessment and Comparative Analysis of the Effectiveness of Leading Generative AI Models
AN  - 3358628142
AB  - While general-purpose large language models (LLMs) represent a new frontier in cybersecurity, their effectiveness for specialized tasks such as phishing detection requires further empirical validation. This dissertation addresses the research gap through a comparative empirical analysis of three leading models: Google Gemini, OpenAI GPT, and Anthropic Claude. Utilizing a balanced dataset of emails, the study evaluated classification performance based on precision, recall, and F1-scores. Quantitative results reveal a critical trade-off in performance. At the same time, models like Claude and Gemini achieved perfect or near-perfect recall (identifying phishing emails). However, they exhibited low precision by incorrectly flagging many legitimate emails as malicious. Claude emerged as the most robust performer with an overall accuracy of 96.1%. Qualitative analysis revealed a trust–verify paradox, where users value LLMs as diagnostic assistants but remain skeptical of black-box outputs, demanding evidence-based reasoning and explainability. This research concludes that LLMs serve as a valuable secondary advisory tool for enhancing user vigilance but are not ready for autonomous use.
