TY  - THES
T1  - Enhancing Cybersecurity Threat Detection With Generative Models: Data Augmentation and Ensemble Learning Using Variational Autoencoders and GANs
AN  - 3382998387
AB  - The evolving landscape of cyber threats, particularly malware and malicious URLs, presents critical challenges for cybersecurity systems that rely on traditional detection methods. This thesis explores the application of generative artificial intelligence, specifically Variational Autoencoders (VAEs), Conditional VAEs (CVAEs), and Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs), to enhance threat detection capabilities through synthetic data generation and hybrid classification frameworks. Across three studies, generative models are employed to augment datasets for malware and URL classification tasks, addressing key limitations such as data scarcity, class imbalance, and inadequate sample diversity.In the first study, VAEs are used to generate synthetic malware samples, which are then utilized to train machine learning classifiers including Random Forest, XGBoost, and sequential models. The augmented datasets lead to notable improvements in accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-scores, particularly in detecting diverse malware classes. The second study introduces a hybrid classification framework that integrates CVAEs with classifiers such as Support Vector Machine, Random Forest, Gradient Boosting Classifier, and neural networks. This approach enhances malware detection performance while addressing scalability and real-time response requirements. The third study targets the challenges of malicious URL detection, proposing a hybrid data augmentation framework combining VAEs and GANs. The enriched datasets improve detection of underrepresented threats like phishing and defacement URLs, achieving up to a 4% increase in accuracy compared to baseline models. Collectively, these studies demonstrate that generative models significantly enhance the robustness and adaptability of machine learning classifiers in cybersecurity applications. By improving generalization to emerging threats and reducing reliance on limited real-world datasets, this thesis provides a foundation for developing scalable, resilient, and adaptive cyber threat detection systems.
