TY  - THES
T1  - Understanding and Managing Integrated Advanced Persistent Threats: Evaluation and Analysis of Large Language Models for Phishing Detection in Small and Midsize Enterprises
AN  - 3232888722
AB  - In 2024, the proliferation of Generative Artificial Intelligence(GAI) led to a surge in innovation and practical application, but it has also raised concerns about security risks. Cybercriminals thrive in the underground domain of the dark web ecosystem, powered by cutting-edge technology and all sorts of Cybercrime-as-a-Service weaponry such as AI for Social Engineering attacks. This complete dark supply chain offers a diverse array of vicious yet non-tech savvy friendly services, from Phishing to Malware. This can significantly lower the barriers to entry for even non-tech savvy person to launch IAPT attacks against large targets such as governments and corporations, as social engineering is the only required skill which now can be augmented by Large Language Models. However, the current academic research lacks a comprehensive understanding of such arsenal of Integrated Advanced Persistent Threats (IAPT) within the dark web ecosystem. Furthermore, there is a lack of a benchmark on Generative Artificial Intelligence against Social Engineering attack. To address this issue, evaluating the capability of different Large Language Models (LLMs) in detecting phishing attacks is crucial. This study will discover the attack chain, highlight notable CaaS providers and techniques, and discuss possible defense strategies with the consideration of small to medium-sized enterprises (SMEs). This study aims to benchmark and compare the performance of various LLMs against suspicious emails that can bypass traditional content filtering systems (e.g., email gateway, anti-spam). The proposed approach involves using high quality sample email datasets representing different schemes, including human and AI generated emails to evaluate the phishing detection capability of a large selection of popular LLMs using a well-established framework. The results indicate that a few benchmark winning models outperform other models and are potentially production ready. Additionally, the study found that LLMs can provide explanations and suggestions in the output, which can assist real-world users in making decisions. Both proprietary and open-source models are capable of providing accurate detection with comprehensive explanation.The findings suggest that the defensive use of LLMs on phishing detection is not only feasible but also capable on real-world application to prevent Social Engineering attacks from cybercriminals with certain limitations and risks. This study provides a valuable contribution to the field by offering a benchmarking framework for evaluating LLMs against phishing attacks and highlighting the potential use of GAI in enhancing security measures.
