TY  - THES
T1  - Evaluating the Effectiveness of Existing Phishing Detectors on AI Generated Phishing Emails
AN  - 3217981757
AB  - This research evaluates the effectiveness of phishing detection models when faced with phishing emails rephrased by large language models (LLMs). The findings show that LLM-generated rephrased emails impact detection accuracy, though the extent varies by detection method and rephrasing model. BERT-based models experienced a 3–4% drop in performance, while traditional machine learning models using Word2Vec and Stylometric features saw reductions between 7–12%, though results fluctuated depending on the dataset and model. Retraining with rephrased datasets led to modest improvements, with the most significant gains occurring when multiple datasets were used rather than a single source. Training on the Llama2-rephrased dataset provided the greatest accuracy boost, particularly for Word2Vec-based models, though improvements carried over to other rephrased datasets as well. However, increasing training epochs or rephrasing emails multiple times did not yield significant additional benefits. These findings emphasize that exposure to diverse LLM-rephrased phishing emails is key to improving detection models. As AI-generated phishing techniques continue to evolve, adaptive training strategies and frequent model updates will be necessary to maintain detection accuracy.
