TY  - THES
T1  - Investigating Persuasion Techniques in Social Engineering Cybercrime
AN  - 3240534248
AB  - Attackers manipulate potential victims by evoking strong emotional responses, creating a sense of urgency from perceived threats of losing personal possessions or promising an enticing reward to prompt impulsive actions without properly considering the consequences. These tactics and psychological manipulation strategies draw upon principles of persuasion introduced by Robert Cialdini and propaganda techniques, making phishing attacks highly effective. Understanding these principles is crucial to identifying the traits that make phishing emails so effective. An attacker can use multiple persuasive techniques at once to reach his end goal which is emotionally manipulating and influencing victims of different demographics and background knowledge into falling for a persuasive phishing and extortion scheme. To tackle this, we focused on implementing a multi-label persuasion detection system for social engineering attacks. Our research examines two interconnected aspects of cybersecurity vulnerability. Firstly, we address the human element, often considered the weakest link in the security chain, by conducting experiments to analyze social engineering cybercrimes such as phishing and extortion attacks. This involves assessing user susceptibility and behaviors when exposed to these threats. Secondly, we investigate machine learning (ML) vulnerabilities in natural language processing (NLP), focusing on large language model (LLM) attacks such as Unicode homograph textual attacks and jailbreaking prompts. By addressing both human and machine vulnerabilities, our study aims to enhance cybersecurity defenses against evolving online threats. In addition to injecting jailbreaking prompts into Generative AI models to craft new phishing attacks, we leverage generative adversarial networks (GAN) to produce synthetic data, thereby augmenting imbalanced datasets and balancing the training process. We also integrate a machine translation (MT) approach to further enhance data diversity and mitigate the impact of imbalanced data on ML algorithms, improving the overall performance of classification models. Thus, to sum up, we evaluate how attackers employ persuasion and propaganda techniques to craft emotionally manipulative, cohesive attacks. Using a set of previously proposed techniques as well as statistical analyses, we propose an automated multi-label system for persuasion and propaganda detection in harmful content, including phishing, extortion, deception, and hate speech.
