TY  - THES
T1  - Smishing (SMS Phishing) Attacks and Defenses: Current Concerns, Campaign Infrastructures, and Towards a Scoring-Based Defense System
AN  - 3090094891
AB  - Smishing (SMS phishing) is a growing cybersecurity threat that tricks mobile users into revealing sensitive information via deceptive text messages. This thesis presents a study on the impacts of the latest technological advances such as generative AI on smishing attacks, a data-driven understanding of the popular smishing campaigns, web infrastructures such as domain names used, and finally presents a novel scoring- based smishing detection system based on various smish indicator of compromise. The research analyzes publicly available SMS phishing data. The thesis also visualizes relationships within a cluster of messages and clusters are connected via common web infrastructures forming bigger campaigns. A comprehensive dataset is curated, incorporating cleansed messages, URLs, sender information, Gemini AI responses, urgency scores, grammar and spell-checking results, and IP Quality Score data. A novel SMS Scorer system is developed which provides a Smish Score (0-100) to assess the likelihood of a message being a smishing attempt where less score indicates benign or legitimate messages and higher scores initiate more chance of being a smish message. The Smish Score is evaluated compared to Generative AI (like Gemini, ChatGPT) outputs to detect smishes demonstrating its advantages in providing a granular and user-configurable approach to detect smishing threats, whereas generative AIs tend not to confirm smishing attempts or hallucinates, incapable of verifying phishing urls and many times failing to provide any output. SMS Scorer smishing defense system complemented Generative AI (Gemini) obtaining 92% accuracy and excellent recall of 98% at a threshold of 0.40 on 0-1.0 scale of smish score.
